---
title: "SSRM View Subscription"
enterprise: true
---

With the Server-Side Row Model, the `viewSubscription` grid option tells your application which loaded rows are in view, so the server only pushes updates for those rows.

View subscription requires the `ViewSubscriptionModule` and [Row IDs](./server-side-model-configuration/#providing-row-ids).

The example below loads 1,000 stocks in blocks over a simulated websocket, which also streams price updates for the subscribed stocks only. The updates are applied with [Asynchronous Transactions](./server-side-model-updating-transactions/#asynchronous-updates). Note the following:

- Only the loaded rows in view are subscribed. Scrolling to rows in a new block subscribes them once the block has loaded.
- **Subscribed rows**, **Last change** and **Updates sent** count as in the [Client-Side Row Model example](./data-update-view-subscription/).
- Clearing **Subscription active** sets `viewSubscriptionActive` to `false`, which unsubscribes every row and stops the stream. Checking it subscribes the rows in view again.

{% gridExampleRunner title="View Subscription with the Server-Side Row Model" name="ssrm-flat-view-subscription" exampleHeight=615 /%}

View subscription works the same way as with the Client-Side Row Model, so start with [View Subscription](./data-update-view-subscription/) for:

- [Handlers](./data-update-view-subscription/#handlers): what `subscribeRows` and `unsubscribeRows` receive, and in what order.
- [Lifecycle](./data-update-view-subscription/#lifecycle): starting, pausing and stopping the subscription.
- [Excluded Rows](./data-update-view-subscription/#excluded-rows): rows that are never passed to the handlers.
- [Debouncing](./data-update-view-subscription/#debouncing): waiting for scrolling to stop before calling the handlers.

This page covers what is different with the Server-Side Row Model: rows that load in blocks, group rows loaded from the server, and aggregated values the server calculates.

## Loading and Refreshing

A row is subscribed once it is in view and its block has loaded. Loading rows and rows whose block failed to load are never passed to the handlers. When the grid is ready, `subscribeRows` is called with an empty array, as no block has loaded yet.

A row's values come from its block, which can have loaded before the row came into view. When a row is subscribed, have the server send its current values first, then stream its changes.

- A [refresh](./server-side-model-updating-refresh/) without a purge keeps the rows whose IDs are unchanged. A kept row stays subscribed, even when its data changes.
- A refresh with `purge: true` unsubscribes every row, then subscribes them again as they reload.
- Rows removed by a refresh or a [transaction](./server-side-model-updating-transactions/) are unsubscribed, and added rows in view are subscribed once loaded.

## Group and Tree Rows

Rows have the same `type` values as with the [Client-Side Row Model](./data-update-view-subscription/#row-types), with these differences:

- `'group'`: a group row the server loaded, with its `data`. [`getRowId`](./server-side-model-configuration/#providing-row-ids) is called for group rows too, so give them an ID from their route.
- `'tree'`: a row in [Tree Data](./server-side-model-tree-data/). `hasChildren` is `true` for expandable rows, and only expandable rows have a `route`.

To apply an update to a subscribed row, pass its `parentKeys` as the `route` of [`applyServerSideTransactionAsync`](./server-side-model-updating-transactions/#asynchronous-updates). Store them when the row is subscribed, so updates that arrive later can still find the row's store.

### Grouping on the Server {% enterpriseIcon /%} {% #grouping-on-the-server %}

The example below groups stocks by sector on the server. The server sends each sector row with its aggregated values, and loads a sector's stocks when it is expanded. Note the following:

- With every sector collapsed, only the 5 sector rows are subscribed. The server recalculates a subscribed sector row whenever one of its stocks moves, and sends the updated row.
- Expanding a sector loads its stocks, and subscribes the ones in view once their block has loaded.

{% gridExampleRunner title="View Subscription with Server-Side Row Grouping" name="ssrm-grouping-view-subscription" exampleHeight=615 /%}

For the Client-Side Row Model, see [Row Grouping](./data-update-view-subscription/#row-grouping).

### Tree Data on the Server {% enterpriseIcon /%} {% #tree-data-on-the-server %}

The example below shows a portfolio of regions, sectors and stocks as [Tree Data](./server-side-model-tree-data/), loaded over a simulated websocket that also streams updates for the subscribed rows. Region and sector rows show the total value of their stocks, which the server streams whenever one of those stocks moves. The updates are applied with [Asynchronous Transactions](./server-side-model-updating-transactions/#asynchronous-updates), using each row's `parentKeys` as the route. Note the following:

- Scrolling, expanding groups and loading blocks produce subscribe and unsubscribe batches.
- **Purge** reloads all rows. The old rows are unsubscribed, and the new rows subscribed as they load.
- With **Subscription active** cleared, rows still load over the socket, but no updates stream.

{% gridExampleRunner title="View Subscription with Server-Side Tree Data" name="server-side-view-subscription" exampleHeight=615 /%}

For the Client-Side Row Model, see [Tree Data](./data-update-view-subscription/#tree-data).

## Aggregating on the Server

The server calculates the [aggregated values](./server-side-model-grouping/) of group rows, so the grid does not need the leaf rows to show them. To keep a subscribed group row current, recalculate its aggregated values on the server when its leaf rows change, and send the updated group row.

For example, suppose 100 stocks are grouped into 5 sectors, and every group is collapsed. Only the 5 group rows are in view, so only they are subscribed, and the server sends updates for those 5 rows. The stocks are not loaded until a group is expanded.

The [Grouping on the Server](#grouping-on-the-server) example above does this: with every sector collapsed, only the 5 sector rows are subscribed, and the server sends their recalculated values.

The Client-Side Row Model calculates aggregated values in the grid, so it also needs updates for the leaf rows. See [Group Aggregates](./data-update-view-subscription/#group-aggregates).

## Pivoting on the Server

In [pivot mode](./server-side-model-pivoting/), each row in view is a group row, passed with `type: 'group'`. Each of its cells aggregates the leaf rows under it for one pivot result column. A subscription covers the whole row, including result columns scrolled out of view, so the server sends updates for everything the row aggregates.

Turning pivot mode on or off, or changing the pivot columns, reloads the rows. Every row is unsubscribed, and the reloaded rows are subscribed as their blocks load.

The example below has the same stocks, controls and pausing rules as the [Client-Side Row Model pivoting example](./data-update-view-subscription/#pivoting), with these differences:

- The server groups, pivots and aggregates the stocks, and sends the pivot result fields with each block, so the grid creates the pivot result columns. `getRowId` gives group rows an ID from their route.
- **Streamed rows** shows the number of rows the server sends updates for. The server sends each subscribed group row whole, recalculated whenever one of its stocks moves, so **Streamed rows** equals **Subscribed rows** while no stream is paused.
- The updates are applied with [Asynchronous Transactions](./server-side-model-updating-transactions/#asynchronous-updates), using each row's `parentKeys` as the route.

{% gridExampleRunner title="View Subscription with Server-Side Pivoting" name="ssrm-pivot-view-subscription" exampleHeight=615 /%}

